            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Jesus Christ as the ultimate, innocent, and unblemished sacrificial offering for the sins of humanity, fulfilling the Old Testament types of the Passover lamb and the Suffering Servant.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"John 1:29\",\"Revelation 5:6\",\"1 Corinthians 5:7\",\"Isaiah 53:7\",\"1 Peter 1:19\",\"Exodus 12:1-13\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"God's people, believers, or vulnerable followers requiring the care of the Good Shepherd.\",\"basis\":[\"John 21:15\",\"Luke 10:3\",\"Isaiah 40:11\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The apocalyptic judge and conquering warrior who exercises divine wrath and ultimate victory.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 6:16-17\",\"Revelation 17:14\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"General innocence, meekness, or unresisting submission to persecution or slaughter.\",\"basis\":[\"Jeremiah 11:19\",\"2 Samuel 12:3\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The symbol of the Lamb is overwhelmingly associated with Jesus Christ (Agnus Dei), synthesizing Passover imagery, Levitical atonement sacrifices, and Isaianic prophecy. A secondary, highly common usage refers to believers as sheep or lambs belonging to God's flock.\"}",
